+Stage each consequential beat as expectation/attention → trigger → response → changed attitude. Use original key poses and timing, then prepare the needed views, expressions and hands. Ordinary acting needs deliberate gaze, lids/brows, mouth and body agreement; listeners should react when their response matters. A repeated mouth pulse, outward palm and torso sway do not establish thought. The [supplemental Short study](research/reference-ijvy-v001/analysis.md) shows how small facial changes and distinct body poses can communicate without constant head turns.
 
-## Football-specific research and staging
+Give gestures a purpose: an earpiece touch, phone grip, point and button press require different orientation and contact. Review the entire sleeve-to-fingertip chain, approach and recovery. Choose a hold, snap, pause or overlapping reaction for its comic purpose. Body timing must fit the performance rather than defaulting to slow, eased motion. Reuse character construction separately from the animation of a new scene.
 
-Choose the actual event before developing its treatment. Verify participants, result, important actions, event-specific kits, spatial relationships and what relevant footage shows. Separate verified incidents, disputed reports and invented jokes; do not present a fan theory or parody accusation as an established fact. Cite sources in the video research, and distinguish historical appearance from any deliberately selected cartoon simplification.
+Apply [motion planning](../../pipeline/motion-planning.md) to support transfer, force, contacts, arcs and recovery. The accepted whole-character run/jump example is an option for suitable movement, not a required eight-second test, cast or pose-swap method for every scene. A good action test cannot certify dialogue or listening. Choose a representative sequence from the new story that exposes its actual acting, mechanics, environment and editing risks before extending the construction.
 
-For match action, map the necessary attackers, defenders, keeper, ball and offscreen roles. A corner needs plausible supporting participants as well as taker and receiver, not a mechanically complete 22-player lineup. Do not invent exact historical positions; record representative simplification. Check team identity, screen direction, ball trajectory and readable contact at viewing size. Inspect kicks as coordinated support-foot, pelvis, knee, ankle, boot and ball action; a matching point in one frame is not sufficient.
+## Football environments and action
 
-These domain checks supplement the common workflow. Apply [character construction](../../pipeline/styles/construction.md#character-facing-faces-and-anatomy) for character shots and [sound direction](../../pipeline/sound-planning-method.md) when sound is used. Tool choice follows the actual shot; Blender/FFmpeg and the available voice runtime are current implementations, not the definition of football comedy.
+For event-based stories, verify event-specific appearance, relevant footage and important relationships without inventing exact historic positions. Map the attackers, defenders, keeper, ball and offscreen roles needed to explain the action. Representative simplification is allowed; a mechanical 22-player quota is unnecessary.
 
-## Applying benchmarks to every new video
+Build pitch, goal, net depth, penalty-area lines, byline, touchline, corner flags, ball and cast in one coordinate system before framing. Establish scale in a wide view and retain it across shots; a flag belongs at a corner, not beside the nearest visible goalpost. Use perspective, grounding, occlusion, contact shadows and relevant layers to create depth around drawn characters. Reuse the same physical set for the same location and its reveal/reverse angles.
 
-For physical comedy, analyze the cause, whole-body pose and response visible in the selected action interval. Apply [motion planning](../../pipeline/motion-planning.md); benchmark-like heads and kits do not compensate for unconvincing movement. Retire rejected cycles as sources for new animation until they are re-authored and reviewed. Preserve the existing visual floor during that work.
+Author support, wind-up, visible boot/forehead contact, ball release/trajectory, follow-through and reaction as one event. Coordinate opponents and recipients; do not animate an independent ball path and hope it meets a character. Inspect consecutive frames around actual contact and the whole action at normal speed. Intentional impossible gags need clear cause and readable staging; accidental reversals, sliding and incorrect scale do not count as exaggeration.
 
-Read the active benchmark below alongside this profile. In the existing brief/shot plan, map selected moments from both references to their craft principle, our original application, planned action/camera/sound and eventual rendered proof. Choose relevant lessons rather than copying plots or requiring every device. At the existing staging, sound, animatic and delivery checkpoints, compare actual output with those selected references and record gaps. Unheard audio remains unverified. Snapshot the selected benchmark version with the video's production context; completed videos keep their adopted versions.
+## Sound and presentation
 
-## References and reusable selections
+Direct each line for its moment—outrage, pleading, dry sarcasm, calm authority or restrained frustration. Keep each selected character voice identity consistent while varying performance. Nationality-related accents are preferences where suitable voices exist, not a reason to force an unsupported voice. Preserve the installed voice runtime/models, selected references and cached takes. Retiming alone does not require regeneration.
 
-- **[Active benchmark v002](benchmark-v002.md)**: combined Clásico and Barcelona–Bayern craft requirements, original adaptation and proof at existing checkpoints. [Bayern detailed study/player](research/reference-bayern-v001/review.html) and [analysis](research/reference-bayern-v001/analysis.md) preserve observations and audio evidence limits.
-- [Historical benchmark v001](benchmark-v001.md), [reference analysis](research/reference-analysis.md) and [reference board](research/reference-v001/reference-board.jpg): the initial 442oons craft study. Preserve as evidence; its initial proposed duration does not override a later brief.
-- [Source/creator research](research/source-context.md): source context and evidence limits. The local study movie and reference frames are analysis material, not reusable production artwork or sound.
-- [Camera research from episode 001](videos/001-manchester-derby-2026-09-13/research/camera-planning-audit-v001.md): sourced coverage vocabulary and film-specific proposals; choose coverage for the new story rather than copying every move.
-- [Reusable asset catalogue v001](assets/reuse-v001/README.md): selected voice/brand files, hashes, provenance, review scope and scene-bound character extraction references. New actions, views and lines still need their own review.
